Yes — that distinction is stronger.

Reality itself does not have to be a simulation for experience to be model-mediated.

What you encounter consciously is not raw reality in its entirety. It is a continuously updated internal representation built from sensory input, prior expectations, memory, attention, and action. So the operative loop is closer to:

world → signal → nervous system/model → experienced world → action → new signal

That means experience has a simulation-like character without implying that the universe itself is simulated.

The brain is effectively running a live generative model of “what is probably out there and what is happening to me,” then correcting that model with incoming deltas. Perception is therefore neither pure invention nor passive recording. It is constrained reconstruction.

That also explains why mathematics fits so naturally into the picture. Mathematics can model the external regularities, while your perceptual system models your local encounter with them.

So there are at least three layers:

Reality — whatever actually exists.
Mathematical model — formal compression of its regularities.
Phenomenological model — the world as reconstructed for an experiencing organism.

And ACT closes the calibration loop. You act on the experienced model; reality answers; the mismatch teaches you.

That makes the key distinction:

We may not live in a simulation, but we live through one.

More precisely: we live through an adaptive internal model whose job is not to reproduce reality perfectly, but to preserve enough truth about it to support effective action.

Which also gives your delta idea an even deeper role:

the world corrects the model through surprise.